s no article, but that it replaces it when necessary with appropriate words: here is an example of that!\par }{\b\f210 2. Br}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 vibus cr}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 ribus}{\f208 : ablative (pl. 3}{\f208\super rd}{\f208  declension) which is shows that }{\i\f211 short legs}{\f208  are a specific quality of the dog (}{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nem}{\f208 ): it is the ablative of quality.\par }{\b\f210 3. Asp}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 ciens}{\f208 : present participle of }{\b\f210 asp}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 cio}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 ere}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to behold}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 4. V}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 lo}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 as}{\f208 , -a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to fly}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 pr}{\f208 ae}{\b\f210 ter}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 along}{\f208  (with a second sense of }{\i\f211 except}{\f208 ). Hence }{\b\f210 t}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 rram praetervol}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to fly low}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 to hedgehop}{\f208 ). We can also say }{\b\f210 dem}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 ss\u233\'8e vol}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208  (P. 2).\par }{\b\f210 5. Pl}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 et}{\f208 : future of }{\b\f210 pl}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 it}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 it is raining}{\f208 ), an impersonal verb. }{\i\f211 Rain}{\f208  is }{\b\f210 pl}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 via}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 ae}{\f208 , f., for rain }{\i\f211 in general}{\f208 , but i}{\b\f210 mber}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 bris}{\f208 , m., }{\i\f211 downpour}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 falling}{\f208  or }{\i\f211 impending rain}{\f208 . This latter word is masculine (m.) and belongs to the 3}{\f208\super rd}{\f208  declension.\par }{\b\f210 6. V}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ro}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 as}{\f208 , -a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to devour}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 v}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ror}{\f208 , -a}{\b\f210 ris}{\f208 , -a}{\b\f210 ri}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to be devoured}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 vor}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 rer}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 vorar}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 ris}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 vorar}{\f208 e}{\b\f210