.\par 6.\emdash  The doctor is eating a roast hen.\par 7.\emdash  Tell me the truth! Can the doctor cure him?\par 8.\emdash  What do you[pl.] think of crazy stories?\par 9.\emdash  Not all crazy stories are funny (}{\i\f211 laughable}{\f208 ).\par \par NB.\emdash  }{\b\f210 F}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 o}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 fis}{\f208  (infinitive }{\b\f210 f}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 eri}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 to be made}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to become}{\f208 , is used as the passive of }{\b\f210 f}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 cio}{\f208 , -}{\b\i\f212 is}{\f208 , -}{\b\i\f212 ere}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 to make}{\f208  (P. 2).\par \par We reviewed the 1}{\f208\super st}{\f208  declension in lesson 22. Today we are going to bring some order to our knowledge of the second. This declension includes nouns in -}{\b\f210 us}{\f208 , which are masculine with some rare exceptions (such as }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 thodus}{\f208  from lesson 13, which is feminine), nouns in -}{\b\f210 er}{\f208 , equally masculine, and nouns in -}{\b\f210 um}{\f208 , which are all neuter. It also comprises adjectives in -}{\b\f210 us}{\f208  or -}{\b\f210 er}{\f208  in the masculine and -}{\b\f210 um}{\f208  in the neuter (and whose feminine is in -}{\b\f210 a}{\f208 ).\par \par }{\b\i\f212 The genitive of this declension is in}{\b\f210  }{\f208 -}{\b\f210 i.\par }{\f208 \par 1}{\f208\super  st}{\f208  Let's look at masculine nouns in -}{\b\f210 er}{\f208  and in -}{\b\f210 us}{\f208  to start with (}{\i\f211 the generous smith}{\f208 ):\par \par }{\i\f211 \tab \tab Singular}{\f208 :\tab \tab \tab \tab }{\i\f211 Plural}{\f208 :\par nom.\tab \tab FAB}{\b\f210 e}{\f208 R GENEROS}{\b\f210 us}{\f208 \tab \tab FABR}{\b\f210 i}{\f208  GENEROS}{\b\f210 i}{\f208 \par acc.\tab \tab FABR}{\b\f210 um}{\f208  GENEROS}{\b\f210 um}{\f208 \tab \tab FABR}{\b\f210 os}{\f208  GENEROS}{\b\f210 os}{\f208 \par gen.\tab \tab FABR}{\b\f210 i}{\f208  GENEROS}{\b\f210 i}{\f208 \tab \tab \tab FABR}{\b\f210 orum}{\f208  GENEROS}{\b